From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000027 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: flax <flax@tiogapass>
Date: Wed, 10 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fbtp: give the VR vout sensors a usable IPMI range

Every pmbus voltage sensor inherits MaxValue = 255 / MinValue = 0 from
the dbus-sensors default table (PSUSensorMain.cpp:1117,
{"in", PSUProperty("Output Voltage", 255, 0, 3, 0)}).  IPMI encodes an
analog sensor as a single byte spanning MinValue..MaxValue, so that is
exactly one volt per count - and every VR rail on this board sits
between 0.9 V and 1.9 V.  The result is that `ipmitool sensor list`
reports a 1.05 V rail and its 1.15 V critical threshold as the same
number:

  MB VR PCH PVNN O | 1.000 | Volts | ... LowerNC 1.000 ... UpperC 1.000

while the ADC sensor for the same rail, which has a sane range, reads
  MB PVNN PCH STB  | 0.994 | Volts | ...  0.760  ...        1.101

Threshold *detection* is unaffected - dbus-sensors compares the raw
double in Thresholds.cpp:392 and never consults MaxValue - so this is a
reporting defect, not a monitoring gap.  But every IPMI-only consumer
(ipmitool, the SDR, IPMI-sourced SEL text) sees the quantised value.

dbus-sensors honours a per-label <label>_Max override
(PSUSensorMain.cpp:706, applied at 757-785).  Set it just above each
rail's upper-critical threshold: 3 V for the VCCIN rails (critical
2.05 V) and 2 V for the rest (criticals 1.1-1.32 V).  That gives
8-12 mV per count instead of 1000 mV.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [platform configuration]
